mode: "100644"
text: "# Replicability & Application\n\nA teardown is only useful if what you extract is **real and transferable.** This is the honesty layer\n(don't learn the wrong lesson) and the action layer (turn the mechanism into your own content).\n\n## The replicability check — is this even a lesson?\n\nBefore extracting a principle, screen the virality for things that aren't about the content at all:\n\n- **Account-size confound** — a creator with millions of followers gets a running start no small\n  account has. Their \"formula\" may just be *reach they already had*. Ask: would this have worked from\n  a 200-follower account? If not, it's not a content lesson you can copy.\n- **Luck / algorithmic variance** — some posts catch a wave for reasons no one can reproduce.\n  Virality has genuine randomness; not every hit has a transferable cause.\n- **One-time moments** — it rode a specific news event, a cultural moment, a first-mover novelty.\n  That window is closed; the \"principle\" doesn't generalize.\n- **Survivorship bias** — you only see the winners. A thousand creators did the same thing and\n  flopped; the one that hit isn't proof the tactic works. Don't reverse-engineer only the survivors.\n- **Sample size** — **one viral post is an anecdote, not a pattern.** A reliable mechanism shows up\n  across *many* viral pieces in a niche. Treat a single-example takeaway as a hypothesis to test, not\n  a law.\n\nIf a piece's success is mostly confound/luck, **say so plainly** and don't manufacture a false\nprinciple. \"This isn't replicable for you, and here's why\" is a more valuable answer than a\nconfident-but-wrong formula.\n\n## Find patterns across many examples\n\nThe trustworthy way to extract principles: tear down **10+ viral pieces in your niche**, then look\nfor the **mechanisms that recur.** A hook style, an emotional trigger, or a structure that shows up\nacross many winners (from different-sized accounts) is a real, transferable lever. A feature that\nappears once is probably noise. Patterns beat anecdotes.\n\nKeep a **swipe file / pattern library**: collect the examples, your teardowns, and the recurring\ndrivers. Over time this becomes *your* playbook of what spreads in *your* corner — far more reliable\nthan generic \"viral tips.\"\n\n## Extract the transferable principle → apply to your niche\n\nTurn the mechanism into a usable instruction:\n\n1. **State the mechanism** in one line (\"relatable 'expectation vs reality' hook + recognition\n   trigger drove the shares\").\n2. **Translate it to your world** — what's *your* niche's version of that hook/trigger? Not the same\n   topic; the same *mechanism* on your subject.\n3. **Hand it to the content skills** to execute (`hook-writer`, `tiktok-script`, `reels-script`,\n   `caption-writer`, `carousel-writer`), in your `voice.md`.\n\nThe output is never \"copy this.\" It's \"the lever here is X; here's X applied to you.\"\n\n## The ethics line — inspiration vs theft\n\nReverse-engineering a **mechanism** and being inspired is fair game and how all creators learn.\n**Copying someone's specific content, script, or creative wholesale** is plagiarism and can infringe\nIP — and it flops anyway because it's derivative. The rule: take the *principle*, add your *own*\nsubstance and angle. If your output is recognizable as a particular creator's piece with the serial\nnumbers filed off, you've crossed the line — go back to mechanism + your own material.\n\n## Measure honestly\n\nJudging \"what's viral/top\" needs numbers. Other people's posts are **visible/native signals**\n(public counts or pasted data). Your own posts on the covered platforms can use **WoopSocial post\nanalytics**. Be clear about that scope, and apply small-sample caution to a single own-post\n(\"overperformed\" ≠ a proven repeatable win).\n"
